# The Supporting Actress
Tamil title: துணை நடிகை

The district newspaper carried a report that a woman in that small town was to be honoured with a ceremonial golden shawl. It was announced that guests from Sofia would attend the function.

On the appointed day, the event began in the library club. The hall was packed, and the visitors from Sofia sat in the front row.

"What is this but another conventional felicitation ceremony?" many seemed to be thinking as they sat listlessly. Signs of boredom were plainly visible on the faces of the audience. Only the schoolgirls present looked enthusiastic.

Sensing the crowd's mood, the chairman of the organizing committee wanted to conclude the function briefly. But the programme before him contained a very long list of speakers. He thought of deleting many names, but each person represented some organization, so he could not do it. Beside the final name were the words, "Will speak in an individual capacity."

"Yes, I can at least remove this fellow's name," the chairman thought and picked up his pencil. But some feeling restrained his hand.

The meeting began. One conventional speech followed another. The members of the audience sat in weary discomfort, their faces contorted as though they had drunk lamp oil. At last the chairman stammered, "Torstoyan Dobrov... will speak... he..."

"What is this? The chairman is faltering!" many thought, staring intently at the stage.

A colonel in military uniform rose.

"I am afraid I may have to speak for a long time. But I have come all this way for that purpose. So, comrades, please listen to my address until the end."

He spoke in a calm, unhurried voice.

"I came here to tell you of an incident in my life during the period when I was in hiding under the Nazi occupation. In the spring of 1943, while returning from a journey, I realized that a secret-police agent was following me. I changed direction and began taking a winding route. But the man behind me was relentless. Another man in plain clothes was with him. When I reached the edge of the town, I began running toward the forest, hoping I might escape them there.

"But I heard my pursuers shout, 'Stop! Do not run!' They fired over my head. I understood that their plan was to capture me alive.

"I continued to run. By then, however, the number of people pursuing me had increased. I saw a small solitary house in a secluded spot. I immediately approached it through the garden, entered, and shut the door behind me. What I saw there froze my blood.

"A young woman stood inside, wrapped in a white shawl and holding a book. When she saw me, she cried out in shock. By then, my pursuers had reached the place. The young woman immediately clapped her hand over her own mouth. I saw her do it.

"I drew my revolver and held it ready. I resolved to fight as long as the last drop of blood remained in my body.

"The secret-police agents opened the rear gate and came through, trampling the flowering plants.

"At once, the young woman who lived in the house ran outside and screamed loudly.

"'You brutes! Have you no sense? Is this how you destroy flowering plants? My precious flowers! May the sinners who ruined you die a thousand deaths!'

"The policemen stopped uncertainly when they heard her lamentation.

"'Madam, do not be afraid. Just answer one question, and that will be enough. Did a man run in here a moment ago?' one of the police agents asked.

"'Oh, my beloved! Are you truly dead? Sirs, listen to my tragic story. He was a student, a Frenchman. They called him Gaston. He had begun growing a youthful beard. He always wore long leather boots. Why has my own life still not left me? He loved me more than life itself. My beloved king! Oh! Have you vanished?'

"'Look here, woman! What is this babbling? Did you see any man?'

"'Why are you shouting so wildly, sir? If only my lover were alive now... My love! Apple of my eye! Sir, how could you leave me like this?'

"As she spoke, the woman seized both policemen and began weeping against them.

"'Mad! Thoroughly mad!' the policemen said as they left the place.

"Then she came back into the house. Thinking it improper for me to remain in a house where a young woman lived alone, I decided to leave and opened the garden gate.

"'Wait a moment,' she said to me in a voice overflowing with affection.

"Her gaze held such longing and warmth that I wondered whether this could be the same woman who had behaved like a lunatic before the police. Yes, it was she: the same lovely face, the same white shawl.

"'Why are you standing there? Come inside,' she invited me.

"Unable to bring myself to refuse, I followed her.

"Photographs of many theatrical scenes hung in the room.

"'Are you an actress?' I asked.

"'I cannot call myself an actress. You might call me a supporting actress,' she replied.

"'When I heard you screaming, I truly believed you were mad! You performed your part extraordinarily well.'

"'No, it was nothing of the kind. I have never been satisfied with my ability as an actress. I am only a minor performer who plays small roles. If I had acted onstage as badly as I did before those men today, the theatre would have emptied out,' she said."

The colonel stopped speaking for a moment. Then he said:

"At the time, I failed to kiss that lady's hand. So today, at least, I wish to do it."

Applause thundered through the hall. The speaker kissed the hands of the very woman for whom the golden-shawl ceremony had been arranged. She had always played small parts and had always remained unobtrusively a supporting actress. Even now, her figure had been hidden behind the chairman.

14 March 1965
